+\textit{CGEL}'s distinction between proper names and proper nouns shows
+how such categories relate. Proper names are a semantic category whose
+central cases are expressions \enquote{conventionally adopted as the name
+of a particular entity} \citep[515]{HuddlestonPullum2002}. A proper-noun
+head, as in \mention{Mary} or \mention{Melbourne}, is among their
+properties, though not a necessary one: \mention{the University of
+Manchester} is a name headed by the common noun \mention{university}.
+Proper nouns are a lexical category, the nouns \enquote{specialised to the
+function of heading proper names}, so naming use is among their properties
+\citep[516]{HuddlestonPullum2002}. The lexical category interjection and the semantic category of expressive
+signs are related in the same way: each is anchored in one kind of
+property and counts properties of the other kind in its cluster.
