+The grouping here is \term{expressive signs}, a semantic category
+anchored in conveyed stance: its members' deployment conveys an evaluative
+or affective stance rather than describing it. I use \term{expressive} in
+this narrower sense from here on; a conventional instruction for managing
+interpretation or interaction is non-propositional without necessarily
+expressing such a stance. Like a proper name, the category has a
+cluster. \textcite[166--167]{potts2007} lists six properties of
+expressives, among them independence from descriptive content, perspective
+dependence, and strengthening under repetition. Among the category's
+properties of another kind is membership in the lexical category
+interjection, with the independent or supplementary use that goes with it. For \mention{damn}, the
+semantic properties raise the questions of whose attitude is conveyed and
+whether repeating the form strengthens it, on either side of the category
+boundary.
 
